9. Primary User Stories

1. As a guest on /menu, the user can browse dishes through horizontal category rails and add meals quickly without navigating a vertical mega-grid.
2. As a user customizing a dish, the user can open a DishQuickViewSheet, review collapsed nutrition information, select valid accompaniments, and add the configured dish to the cart.
3. As a user reviewing the CartDrawer, the user sees a mandatory CompleteYourMealRail before the single Checkout CTA.
4. As an unauthenticated user accessing a protected account route, the user is redirected to authentication and returned to the exact original route afterward.
5. As a user configuring /plan/[planId], the user sees the exact day-by-day meal lineup before checkout.
6. As a user using Shuffle, meals marked Keep remain unchanged while unlocked meals regenerate.
7. As a user manually replacing a dish, the user’s choice clears the plan-level Undo state.
8. As a user changing duration, mealtime, goal, dietary pattern, or location, the user receives a warning when the change would erase manual meal customization.
9. As a user on /custom-build, the user follows a six-step questionnaire and receives a generated meal lineup without selecting every meal manually.
10. As a user reviewing a custom plan, the user sees meal count, total price, original price where applicable, savings, and effective price per meal.
11. As a user on /checkout, the user always sees the exact amount payable today and future renewal amount.
12. As a user submitting payment, repeated submission or network retry cannot create duplicate payment, order, or subscription records.
13. As an active subscriber on /meal-planner, the user can view delivery dates, cutoff times, and available meal-management actions.
14. As a subscriber, the user can skip, pause, swap, change slot, or change address where operationally permitted, with unavailable actions explaining why.
15. As a user leaving meal feedback, the user can choose Loved it, It was okay, or Not for me, with optional structured reason tags.
16. As a wearable-connected user, health context may influence explainable recommendations but may never change a meal automatically.
17. As a corporate employee, an invite and entitlement are validated server-side and reflected consistently throughout plan configuration and checkout.
18. As a dietitian-referred user, the user sees verified RD recommendations clearly distinguished from personal preferences.
19. As a user booking an RD consultation, the user can browse profiles, select a valid slot, review the appointment, and complete booking.
20. As a user encountering an expired quote or network failure, the user can safely refresh the quote without losing the configured plan.


